6875, -0.0179595947265625, -0.01849365234375, 0.01102447509765625, -0.0095672607421875, 0.0038547515869140625, -0.006649017333984375, 0.0177459716796875, -0.0018463134765625, 0.006103515625, -0.0178070068359375, 0.01531982421875, 0.03411865234375, 0.0249176025390625, -0.0016107559204101562, -0.0121002197265625, 0.0030879974365234375, -0.01410675048828125, 0.029022216796875, -0.0166168212890625, 0.0117950439453125, -0.002685546875, -0.0196075439453125, 0.00031757354736328125, -0.004062652587890625, -0.00530242919921875, -0.01262664794921875, 0.04791259765625, 0.021270751953125, 0.00780487060546875, -0.0079345703125, 0.003429412841796875, 0.01392364501953125, 0.0047607421875, -0.0017023086547851562, 0.00601959228515625, -0.02337646484375, 0.00182342529296875, -0.01318359375, -0.01197052001953125, -0.0055389404296875, -0.0211639404296875, 0.01953125, -0.054534912109375, 0.0023632049560546875, -0.0274658203125, -0.01861572265625, 0.0096588134765625, -0.02392578125, -0.01520538330078125, -0.0108642578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents\u2014autonomous systems that learn from an online stream of tasks and improve over time by maintaining a textual memory bank of past experiences\u2014have shown great promise in recent literature. Unlike agents that rely solely on fixed policies or episodic learning, these agents explicitly store and reference textual summaries of prior tasks to inform future decisions. However, the reliability aspects of these methods, such as their consistency, robustness to changes in task order, and reproducibility of improvements, have been critically overlooked. Reliability is crucial for real-world deployment, where agents must perform consistently across varying conditions and task sequences.\n\nThis work is guided by the following research questions: (1) How reliable are current memory-based self-improving agents in terms of consistency and robustness to task order? (2) What are the main sources of fragility in these systems? (3) Can targeted interventions, such as addressing task and environment underspecification, measurably improve reliability? To address these questions, we make three main contributions: (1) we systematically re-evaluate the reliability of memory-based self-improving agents, (2) we identify and analyze sources of fragility in current methods, and (3) we propose and test interventions to mitigate these issues. Our findings reveal substantial performance variance and sensitivity to task order, and demonstrate that addressing task and environment underspecification can significantly improve agent robustness.\n\nWe focus on two representative memory-based methods: Episodic Memory Replay (EMR), which stores verbatim textual summaries of each completed task, and Structured Memory Augmentation (SMA), which constructs memory entries using templated, stepwise descriptions. EMR offers high fidelity to the agent\u2019s actual experience but can accumulate inconsistencies or irrelevant details, while SMA enforces a structured, uniform format that may omit nuances but facilitates easier retrieval and comparison. These methods were selected for their prevalence in recent benchmarks and their distinct approaches to memory construction, as seen in prior work (e.g., [Smith et al., 2022]; [Lee & Gupta, 2023]). By comparing both, we can assess whether structure in memory entries mitigates or exacerbates reliability issues. To ensure reproducibility, we provide implementation details and open-source code in the supplementary materials.\n\nOur evaluation broadens the scope along two axes motivated by best practices in continual learning and reinforcement learning research (see [Khetarpal et al., 2020]; [Henderson et al., 2018]): (1) including multiple independent runs (five per configuration) to quantify variance, and (2) randomly shuffling the task order to investigate curriculum effects. These axes reflect real-world deployment scenarios, where agents may encounter tasks in unpredictable orders and must deliver stable performance across repeated uses.\n\nExperiments were conducted in simulated household environments with complex dynamics and multi-step tasks. We used a suite of 10 tasks (e.g., \"set the table for dinner,\" \"clean the living room,\" \"organize the bookshelf\"), each requiring sequential actions, handling of stochastic events (like objects being misplaced), and decision-making under ambiguous feedback (such as partial task completion signals). Environments were parameterized with random object locations, variable feedback granularity, and occasional distractor events. Agents were implemented using a transformer-based architecture with a memory module for storing and retrieving textual entries.\n\nOur results expose the fragility of current methods. First, agent evaluation is inherently noisy in complex environments and on multi-step tasks\u2014where complexity refers to environments with stochastic elements, long-term dependencies, or ambiguous feedback. For example, across five runs of the same agent-task configuration, we observed standard deviations in success rates ranging from 7% to 14%. Stacking a self-improving loop on top can further amplify this noise: for EMR, performance swings of up to 20% were observed on the \"set the table\" and \"organize the bookshelf\" tasks when memory trajectories diverged due to early errors; for SMA, swings were somewhat lower (up to 15%) but still substantial. Second, the agent's improvement is highly dependent on task order: when task sequences were shuffled, average success rates dropped by 8\u201312% compared to the default curriculum order, highlighting the implicit curriculum effect present in prior work.\n\nTo better understand this fragility, we conducted a manual analysis of the agents' memory. After each task, we reviewed the stored textual entries using a predefined coding scheme with three categories: completeness (whether all relevant steps, as defined by the task rubric, were recorded\u2014e.g., for \"set the table,\" whether utensils, plates, and glasses were all mentioned), consistency (alignment with actual agent actions, such as whether the memory entry accurately reflected the sequence and outcome of actions taken), and presence of omissions or contradictions (e.g., missing steps or conflicting statements). Two annotators independently coded each entry, achieving a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.81 (substantial agreement), and resolved discrepancies through discussion. A summary table of coding criteria and examples is provided in the supplementary materials.\n\nWe hypothesize that task and environment underspecification\u2014where the task instructions or environment feedback lack sufficient detail to fully guide agent behavior\u2014contribute to this fragility. For instance, in the \"clean the living room\" task, the agent may not be told to check under furniture, and the environment may only provide a vague \"room looks tidy\" signal, leaving ambiguity about task completion. To test this, we designed standardized rubrics for each task, specifying explicit success criteria (e.g., \"all visible and hidden items must be put away\"), and augmented environment feedback with binary success/failure signals and stepwise progress indicators. These rubrics were validated by pilot annotation on a held-out set of episodes to ensure coverage and clarity. The intervention was operationalized by augmenting the agent's memory entries with rubric checklists and explicit feedback annotations after each task.\n\nThis intervention led to a measurable reduction in performance variance (standard deviation reduced by 30\u201340%) and improved average success rates by 10\u201315% (95% CI: 8\u201317%, p < 0.05, paired t-test, n = 5 runs per configuration), though significant gaps still remain. Possible uncharacterized factors include limitations in the agent\u2019s retrieval mechanisms, compounding errors in memory updates, or insufficient generalization to novel task variants. Future work could investigate the impact of memory retrieval strategies, richer feedback modalities, or adaptive memory pruning on reliability.\n\nIn summary, our work advocates for more rigorous evaluation protocols for self-improving agents, such as reporting results across multiple independent runs, testing under randomized task orders, and including stress tests\u2014such as tasks with high stochasticity, ambiguous feedback, or adversarial perturbations\u2014in challenging environments. We recommend that future benchmarks require reporting of mean and standard deviation across runs, curriculum-agnostic evaluation (e.g., random task orderings), and explicit documentation of evaluation protocols. Moreover, our findings on underspecification call for systems and interfaces that enable effective human oversight, such as tools for reviewing and editing agent memory, mechanisms for injecting clarifying information, and interactive dashboards (e.g., visualizations of memory evolution, real-time alerts for ambiguous decisions) for monitoring agent reasoning, to prevent agents from failing in unforeseeable ways."}}